s for Medicare & Medicaid Services", "source": "federal_register", "summary": "This document corrects technical and typographical errors in the final rule that appeared in the Federal Register on August 4, 2026 titled \\"Medicare Program; Hospital Inpatient Prospective Payment Systems for Acute Care Hospitals (IPPS) and the Long-Term Care Hospital Prospective Payment System and Policy Changes and Fiscal Year (FY) 2027 Rates; Requirements for Quality Programs; Other Policy Changes; and Adoption of Updated Versions of Certain Health Information Technology Standards.\\"", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-09-29T11:00:06.655924+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/09/29/2026-19946/medicare-program-hospital-inpatient-prospective-payment-systems-for-acute-care-hospitals-ipps-and", "external_id": "2026-19946", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services · Federal", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-09-29", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": null}, {"id": "ca761b8d-820e-4282-af2a-9707745742e2", "raw": {"title": "October 2026 Quarterly Update to HCPCS Codes for SNF Consolidated Billing", "agency": "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)", "summary": "CMS updated Healthcare Common Procedure Coding System codes used for Skilled Nursing Facility consolidated billing enforcement, requiring updates to billing systems and coding practices.", "source_url": "https://www.hhs.gov/guidance/?combine=&page=1", "jurisdiction": "Federal - United States", "effective_date": "2026-10-05", "published_date": "2026-09-01", "why_it_applies": "iX Health operates in the healthcare sector and provides medical billing and coding services to healthcare organizations."}, "title": "October 2026 Quarterly Update to HCPCS Codes for SNF Consolidated Billing", "agency": "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "CMS updated Healthcare Common Procedure Coding System codes used for Skilled Nursing Facility consolidated billing enforcement, requiring updates to billing systems and coding practices.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-05T21:54:17.210893+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.hhs.gov/guidance/?combine=&page=1", "external_id": "6c0572f0-c971-4219-aeb2-9ec37339079f:https://www.hhs.gov/guidance/?combine=&page=1", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Federal - United States", "effective_date": "2026-10-05", "published_date": "2026-09-01", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T00:56:37.237+00:00"}, {"id": "3de1ee07-489f-4aa4-b427-b22d78e061a1", "raw": {"title": "Commission Delegated Act on Portable Battery Removal Exemptions", "agency": "European Commission, Directorate-General for Environment", "summary": "Introduces new exemptions to EU requirements on removability and replaceability of portable batteries for six additional product categories.", "source_url": "https://environment.ec.europa.eu/news/commission-adds-exemptions-portable-battery-removal-rules-2026-07-14_en", "jurisdiction": "European Union", "effective_date": "", "published_date": "2026-07-14", "why_it_applies": "Rheintal Batterie GmbH (test) is a battery manufacturing company, and this regulation affects battery module design requirements."}, "title": "Commission Delegated Act on Portable Battery Removal Exemptions", "agency": "European Commission, Directorate-General for Environment", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "Introduces new exemptions to EU requirements on removability and replaceability of portable batteries for six additional product categories.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-08T22:58:58.322985+00:00", "source_url": "https://environment.ec.europa.eu/news/commission-adds-exemptions-portable-battery-removal-rules-2026-07-14_en", "external_id": "1ae65692-f23c-4109-8064-72a34d27f2be:https://environment.ec.europa.eu/news/commission-adds-exemptions-portable-battery-removal-rules-2026-07-14_en", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "European Union", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-07-14", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T23:08:02.497+00:00"}, {"id": "8e2859cd-f970-42dc-8611-66da06df3e7c", "raw": {"type": "Notice", "title": "Initiation of Second Four-Year Review Process: China''s Acts, Policies, and Practices Related to Technology Transfer, Intellectual Property, and Innovation", "pdf_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-05-06/pdf/2026-08806.pdf", "abstract": "The U.S. Trade Representative is commencing the second, statutory four-year review of the two actions taken under Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974, as amended (Trade Act), in the investigation of China''s Acts, Policies, and Practices Related to Technology Transfer, Intellectual Property, and Innovation. The two actions were effective, respectively, on July 6, 2018, and August 23, 2018, and subsequently were modified by imposing additional duties on supplemental lists of products, by the temporary removal of duties on certain products through product exclusions, and through the first, statutory four-year review. The first step in the four-year review process is notifying representatives of domestic industries which benefit from the actions of the possible termination of the actions and of the opportunity for these representatives to request continuation of the actions. Requests for continuation must be received in the 60-day window prior to the second four-year anniversary of the respective action: (1) between May 7, 2026, and July 5, 2026, for the July 6, 2018, action; and (2) between June 24, 2026, and August 22, 2026, for the August 23, 2018, action. The Office of the United States Trade Representative (USTR) is notifying interested parties of these two time windows for representatives of domestic industries which benefit from the trade actions to request continuation of the corresponding trade actions through the USTR website portal. If the trade actions continue as a result of one or more requests from representatives of domestic industries which benefit from the actions, USTR will proceed with the next phase of the review of the trade actions. The second phase of the review would be announced in one or more subsequent notices and would provide opportunities for public comments from all interested parties.", "agencies": [{"id": 491, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/trade-representative-office-of-united-states", "name": "Trade Representative, Office of United States", "slug": "trade-representative-office-of-united-states", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/491", "raw_name": "OFFICE OF THE UNITED STATES TRADE REPRESENTATIVE", "parent_id": null}], "excerpts": "The U.S. Trade Representative is commencing the second, statutory four-year review of the two actions taken under Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974, as amended (Trade Act), in the investigation of China''s Acts, Policies, and Practices Related to...", "html_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/05/06/2026-08806/initiation-of-second-four-year-review-process-chinas-acts-policies-and-practices-related-to", "raw_text_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/full_text/text/2026/05/06/2026-08806.txt", "document_number": "2026-08806", "publication_date": "2026-05-06", "public_inspection_pdf_url": "https://public-inspection.federalregister.gov/2026-08806.pdf?1777985114"}, "title": "Initiation of Second Four-Year Review Process: China''s Acts, Policies, and Practices Related to Technology Transfer, Intellectual Property, and Innovation", "agency": "Trade Representative, Office of United States", "source": "federal_register", "summary": "The U.S. Trade Representative is commencing the second, statutory four-year review of the two actions taken under Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974, as amended (Trade Act), in the investigation of China''s Acts, Policies, and Practices Related to Technology Transfer, Intellectual Property, and Innovation. The two actions were effective, respectively, on July 6, 2018, and August 23, 2018, and subsequently were modified by imposing additional duties on supplemental lists of products, by the temporary removal of duties on certain products through product exclusions, and through the first, statutory four-year review. The first step in the four-year review process is notifying representatives of domestic industries which benefit from the actions of the possible termination of the actions and of the opportunity for these representatives to request continuation of the actions. Requests for continuation must be received in the 60-day window prior to the second four-year anniversary of the respective action: (1) between May 7, 2026, and July 5, 2026, for the July 6, 2018, action; and (2) between June 24, 2026, and August 22, 2026, for the August 23, 2018, action. The Office of the United States Trade Representative (USTR) is notifying interested parties of these two time windows for representatives of domestic industries which benefit from the trade actions to request continuation of the corresponding trade actions through the USTR website portal. If the trade actions continue as a result of one or more requests from representatives of domestic industries which benefit from the actions, USTR will proceed with the next phase of the review of the trade actions. The second phase of the review would be announced in one or more subsequent notices and would provide opportunities for public comments from all interested parties.", "category": "geopolitical", "countries": ["China"], "created_at": "2026-10-04T02:29:09.161622+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/05/06/2026-08806/initiation-of-second-four-year-review-process-chinas-acts-policies-and-practices-related-to", "external_id": "2026-08806", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Trade Representative, Office of United States · Federal", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-05-06", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": null}, {"id": "453b294b-0d15-4480-bc9b-35847ea17ad7", "raw": {"title": "Notice of Privacy Practices (NPP) - HIPAA Privacy Rule Modifications", "agency": "U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Office for Civil Rights (OCR)", "summary": "HIPAA covered entities and business associates must update their Notice of Privacy Practices to reflect certain permissible uses of protected health information, with specific provisions related to reproductive health invalidated by court order.", "source_url": "https://www.konfirmity.com/blog/hipaa-what-changed-in-2026", "jurisdiction": "Federal - United States", "effective_date": "2026-02-16", "published_date": "", "why_it_applies": "iX Health operates in the healthcare sector, handles health/patient information, and operates as a business associate for healthcare providers."}, "title": "Notice of Privacy Practices (NPP) - HIPAA Privacy Rule Modifications", "agency": "U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Office for Civil Rights (OCR)", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "HIPAA covered entities and business associates must update their Notice of Privacy Practices to reflect certain permissible uses of protected health information, with specific provisions related to reproductive health invalidated by court order.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-05T21:54:16.032976+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.konfirmity.com/blog/hipaa-what-changed-in-2026", "external_id": "6c0572f0-c971-4219-aeb2-9ec37339079f:https://www.konfirmity.com/blog/hipaa-what-changed-in-2026", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Federal - United States", "effective_date": "2026-02-16", "published_date": null, "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T00:56:37.237+00:00"}, {"id": "71ccab91-803d-42e3-b60d-6290c0e17287", "raw": {"title": "Pipeline Safety: Repair Criteria for Hazardous Liquid and Gas Transmission Pipelines", "agency": "Pipeline and Hazardous Materials Safety Administration (PHMSA), U.S. Department of Transportation", "summary": "PHMSA proposes to modernize and clarify the anomaly response criteria in the Federal pipeline safety regulations for gas transmission and hazardous liquid pipelines, which would affect inspection and maintenance service providers.", "source_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/pipeline-and-hazardous-materials-safety-administration", "jurisdiction": "Federal (United States)", "effective_date": "", "published_date": "2026-07-08", "why_it_applies": "Cascade Pipeline Services (test) provides pipeline inspection, leak detection and repair services in the energy/infrastructure sector in Texas, Louisiana, and Oklahoma."}, "title": "Pipeline Safety: Repair Criteria for Hazardous Liquid and Gas Transmission Pipelines", "agency": "Pipeline and Hazardous Materials Safety Administration (PHMSA), U.S. Department of Transportation", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "PHMSA proposes to modernize and clarify the anomaly response criteria in the Federal pipeline safety regulations for gas transmission and hazardous liquid pipelines, which would affect inspection and maintenance service providers.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-08T22:57:31.414768+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/pipeline-and-hazardous-materials-safety-administration", "external_id": "9d88191d-191e-4ac4-b8ce-e02f9a954dee:https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/pipeline-and-hazardous-materials-safety-administration", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Federal (United States)", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-07-08", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T23:08:02.497+00:00"}, {"id": "89fa1064-7170-4cb4-bcf8-9ef630aa066f", "raw": {"title": "Streamlining Export Controls for Drone Exports (Final Rule)", "agency": "US Department of Commerce, Bureau of Industry and Security (BIS)", "summary": "BIS published a final rule further easing export controls on certain UAVs and related hardware, technology and software under the Export Administration Regulations, substantially relaxing EAR controls on ordinary civilian and commercial drones while retaining controls for military-designed systems.", "source_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-08-14/pdf/2026-16628.pdf", "jurisdiction": "United States", "effective_date": "2026-08-13", "published_date": "2026-08-14"}, "title": "Streamlining Export Controls for Drone Exports (Final Rule)", "agency": "US Department of Commerce, Bureau of Industry and Security (BIS)", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "BIS published a final rule further easing export controls on certain UAVs and related hardware, technology and software under the Export Administration Regulations, substantially relaxing EAR controls on ordinary civilian and commercial drones while retaining controls for military-designed systems.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-02T22:08:14.370168+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-08-14/pdf/2026-16628.pdf", "external_id": "51973b66-f862-464c-9a81-abdc54bbfd7c:https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-08-14/pdf/2026-16628.pdf", "duplicate_of": "b9e8c7fa-ad74-4b14-82b0-9de152ac93f9", "jurisdiction": "United States", "effective_date": "2026-08-13", "published_date": "2026-08-14", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T00:56:37.237+00:00"}, {"id": "e4202c28-7ab7-433e-badc-c2c1e7ef12cd", "raw": {"feed": "https://www.legislation.gov.uk/new/uksi/data.feed"}, "title": "The Employment Rights Act 2025 (Commencement No. 6 and Transitional and Saving Provisions) Regulations 2026", "agency": "UK Parliament (statutory instruments)", "source": "uk_legislation", "summary": "These Regulations bring into force certain provisions of the Employment Rights Act 2025 (c. 36) (“the 2025 Act”) on 30th October 2026. They are the sixth commencement regulations made under the 2025 Act. Certain provisions came into force on Royal Assent by virtue of section 159 (1) of the 2025 Act and two months after Royal Assent by virtue of section 159 (2) of the 2025 Act.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-06T21:00:20.766845+00:00", "source_url": "http://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2026/1065/made", "external_id": "/uksi/2026/1065/made", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "United Kingdom", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-10-06", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T00:56:37.15+00:00"}, {"id": "95b3e0f8-0f20-411a-b925-8403ccb2b316", "raw": {"type": "Rule", "title": "Air Plan Approval; FL; Emissions Reporting Requirements and Permitting Forms", "pdf_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-08-27/pdf/2026-17490.pdf", "abstract": "The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) is approving a State Implementation Plan (SIP) revision submitted by the Florida Department of Environmental Protection (FDEP) on August 15, 2023. The revision updates reporting requirements; adds, updates, and renames forms for several permit applications; renumbers and updates the effective dates of various forms to align with programmatic changes; and improves the process for submitting forms across several rules within the Florida SIP. Additionally, the revision removes a rule concerning administrative permit corrections from the SIP. The EPA is approving these changes pursuant to the Clean Air Act (CAA or Act).", "agencies": [{"id": 145, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/environmental-protection-agency", "name": "Environmental Protection Agency", "slug": "environmental-protection-agency", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/145", "raw_name": "ENVIRONMENTAL PROTECTION AGENCY", "parent_id": null}], "excerpts": "The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) is approving a State Implementation Plan (SIP) revision submitted by the Florida Department of Environmental Protection (FDEP) on August 15, 2023. The revision updates reporting requirements; adds,...", "html_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/08/27/2026-17490/air-plan-approval-fl-emissions-reporting-requirements-and-permitting-forms", "raw_text_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/full_text/text/2026/08/27/2026-17490.txt", "document_number": "2026-17490", "publication_date": "2026-08-27", "countries_from_full_text": false, "public_inspection_pdf_url": "https://public-inspection.federalregister.gov/2026-17490.pdf?1787748315"}, "title": "Air Plan Approval; FL; Emissions Reporting Requirements and Permitting Forms", "agency": "Environmental Protection Agency", "source": "federal_register", "summary": "The U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) is approving a State Implementation Plan (SIP) revision submitted by the Florida Department of Environmental Protection (FDEP) on August 15, 2023. The revision updates reporting requirements; adds, updates, and renames forms for several permit applications; renumbers and updates the effective dates of various forms to align with programmatic changes; and improves the process for submitting forms across several rules within the Florida SIP. Additionally, the revision removes a rule concerning administrative permit corrections from the SIP. The EPA is approving these changes pursuant to the Clean Air Act (CAA or Act).", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-09-23T01:15:09.498411+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/08/27/2026-17490/air-plan-approval-fl-emissions-reporting-requirements-and-permitting-forms", "external_id": "2026-17490", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Environmental Protection Agency · Federal", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-08-27", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": null}, {"id": "0bc31300-78d2-4daf-a34a-598f9d9fa59c", "raw": {"type": "Proposed Rule", "title": "Commodity Pool Operators and Commodity Trading Advisors: Reduction of Duplicative Regulation Through Intermediary Registration Exemptions; Expansion of the Exemption for Small Commodity Pools", "pdf_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-08-21/pdf/2026-17079.pdf", "abstract": "The Commodity Futures Trading Commission (\\"Commission\\" or \\"CFTC\\") is proposing several amendments to its registration requirements for certain commodity pool operators (\\"CPOs\\") and commodity trading advisors (\\"CTAs\\") to reduce duplicative and overlapping regulation and reflect inflation (\\"Proposal\\"). The Proposal would add an exemption from CPO registration for certain investment advisers registered with the Securities and Exchange Commission (\\"Registered Investment Advisers\\" or \\"RIAs\\") in relation to commodity pools for which the participants are limited to certain sophisticated investors and which meet other conditions; add a related registration exemption for CTAs; and increase the total gross capital contributions threshold in the CPO registration exemption for small commodity pools (commonly referred to as the \\"Small Pool Exemption\\") to account for inflation. The Commission preliminarily intends for the Proposal, if adopted, to supersede certain no-action positions issued by the Commission''s Market Participants Division (\\"MPD\\").", "agencies": [{"id": 77, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/commodity-futures-trading-commission", "name": "Commodity Futures Trading Commission", "slug": "commodity-futures-trading-commission", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/77", "raw_name": "COMMODITY FUTURES TRADING COMMISSION", "parent_id": null}], "excerpts": "The Commodity Futures Trading Commission (\\"Commission\\" or \\"CFTC\\") is proposing several amendments to its registration requirements for certain commodity pool operators (\\"CPOs\\") and commodity trading advisors (\\"CTAs\\") to reduce duplicative and...", "html_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/08/21/2026-17079/commodity-pool-operators-and-commodity-trading-advisors-reduction-of-duplicative-regulation-through", "raw_text_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/full_text/text/2026/08/21/2026-17079.txt", "document_number": "2026-17079", "publication_date": "2026-08-21", "countries_from_full_text": false, "public_inspection_pdf_url": "https://public-inspection.federalregister.gov/2026-17079.pdf?1787229912"}, "title": "Commodity Pool Operators and Commodity Trading Advisors: Reduction of Duplicative Regulation Through Intermediary Registration Exemptions; Expansion of the Exemption for Small Commodity Pools", "agency": "Commodity Futures Trading Commission", "source": "federal_register", "summary": "The Commodity Futures Trading Commission (\\"Commission\\" or \\"CFTC\\") is proposing several amendments to its registration requirements for certain commodity pool operators (\\"CPOs\\") and commodity trading advisors (\\"CTAs\\") to reduce duplicative and overlapping regulation and reflect inflation (\\"Proposal\\"). The Proposal would add an exemption from CPO registration for certain investment advisers registered with the Securities and Exchange Commission (\\"Registered Investment Advisers\\" or \\"RIAs\\") in relation to commodity pools for which the participants are limited to certain sophisticated investors and which meet other conditions; add a related registration exemption for CTAs; and increase the total gross capital contributions threshold in the CPO registration exemption for small commodity pools (commonly referred to as the \\"Small Pool Exemption\\") to account for inflation. The Commission preliminarily intends for the Proposal, if adopted, to supersede certain no-action positions issued by the Commission''s Market Participants Division (\\"MPD\\").", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-09-23T01:15:09.498411+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/08/21/2026-17079/commodity-pool-operators-and-commodity-trading-advisors-reduction-of-duplicative-regulation-through", "external_id": "2026-17079", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Commodity Futures Trading Commission · Federal", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-08-21", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": null}, {"id": "09f5ec50-547d-4313-a51c-078a86a821fc", "raw": {"title": "Regulation of the European Parliament and of the Council establishing the European Defence Industry Programme and a framework of measures to ensure the timely availability and supply of defence products", "agency": "European Commission / European Parliament and Council", "summary": "EDIP is the EU''s first long-term legal framework for joint defense procurement, industrial ramp-up, and supply chain security, with defined eligibility criteria aimed at reducing strategic dependencies and designed to make defense spending more, together, and European.", "source_url": "https://www.aoshearman.com/en/insights/eus-long-term-framework-for-joint-defence-procurement-industrial-ramp-up-and-security-of-supply", "jurisdiction": "European Union", "effective_date": "2025-12-01", "published_date": "2025-11-28"}, "title": "Regulation of the European Parliament and of the Council establishing the European Defence Industry Programme and a framework of measures to ensure the timely availability and supply of defence products", "agency": "European Commission / European Parliament and Council", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "EDIP is the EU''s first long-term legal framework for joint defense procurement, industrial ramp-up, and supply chain security, with defined eligibility criteria aimed at reducing strategic dependencies and designed to make defense spending more, together, and European.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-02T22:08:12.561383+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.aoshearman.com/en/insights/eus-long-term-framework-for-joint-defence-procurement-industrial-ramp-up-and-security-of-supply", "external_id": "51973b66-f862-464c-9a81-abdc54bbfd7c:https://www.aoshearman.com/en/insights/eus-long-term-framework-for-joint-defence-procurement-industrial-ramp-up-and-security-of-supply", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "European Union", "effective_date": "2025-12-01", "published_date": "2025-11-28", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T00:56:37.15+00:00"}, {"id": "46e963e0-e29b-4959-aeea-41957c62983f", "raw": {"type": "Proposed Rule", "title": "Political Contributions by Certain Investment Advisers", "pdf_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-09-10/pdf/2026-18424.pdf", "abstract": "The Securities and Exchange Commission (the \\"Commission\\" or the \\"SEC\\") is proposing to rescind the political contribution rule under the Investment Advisers Act of 1940 (the \\"Advisers Act\\"), which prohibits investment advisers from providing investment advisory services for compensation to a government client for two years after an adviser or any covered associate of the adviser makes a contribution to certain categories of elected officials or candidates, among other prohibitions. In the more than fifteen years since the rule was adopted, implementation challenges associated with the political contribution rule have resulted in a range of significant unintended consequences, including compliance practices among some investment advisers that may have had the effect of restricting all political contributions by the investment advisers and their employees. Market participants also have stated that the political contribution rule is burdensome, complex, and both lacks clarity and creates a de facto strict liability standard. The Commission is of the view that other existing requirements of the Advisers Act and its associated rules, including prohibitions on fraud, fiduciary duty requirements, the compliance rule, and the code of ethics rule (defined below), are likely sufficient to address pay-to-play practices while allowing an adviser the flexibility to implement an approach that is more appropriately tailored to its particular risks, rendering the political contribution rule unnecessary. The Commission also is proposing to amend the rule under the Advisers Act pertaining to books and records consistent with the proposed rescission.", "agencies": [{"id": 466, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/securities-and-exchange-commission", "name": "Securities and Exchange Commission", "slug": "securities-and-exchange-commission", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/466", "raw_name": "SECURITIES AND EXCHANGE COMMISSION", "parent_id": null}], "excerpts": "The Securities and Exchange Commission (the \\"Commission\\" or the \\"SEC\\") is proposing to rescind the political contribution rule under the Investment Advisers Act of 1940 (the \\"Advisers Act\\"), which prohibits investment advisers from providing investment...", "html_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/09/10/2026-18424/political-contributions-by-certain-investment-advisers", "raw_text_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/full_text/text/2026/09/10/2026-18424.txt", "document_number": "2026-18424", "publication_date": "2026-09-10", "countries_from_full_text": false, "public_inspection_pdf_url": "https://public-inspection.federalregister.gov/2026-18424.pdf?1788957911"}, "title": "Political Contributions by Certain Investment Advisers", "agency": "Securities and Exchange Commission", "source": "federal_register", "summary": "The Securities and Exchange Commission (the \\"Commission\\" or the \\"SEC\\") is proposing to rescind the political contribution rule under the Investment Advisers Act of 1940 (the \\"Advisers Act\\"), which prohibits investment advisers from providing investment advisory services for compensation to a government client for two years after an adviser or any covered associate of the adviser makes a contribution to certain categories of elected officials or candidates, among other prohibitions. In the more than fifteen years since the rule was adopted, implementation challenges associated with the political contribution rule have resulted in a range of significant unintended consequences, including compliance practices among some investment advisers that may have had the effect of restricting all political contributions by the investment advisers and their employees. Market participants also have stated that the political contribution rule is burdensome, complex, and both lacks clarity and creates a de facto strict liability standard. The Commission is of the view that other existing requirements of the Advisers Act and its associated rules, including prohibitions on fraud, fiduciary duty requirements, the compliance rule, and the code of ethics rule (defined below), are likely sufficient to address pay-to-play practices while allowing an adviser the flexibility to implement an approach that is more appropriately tailored to its particular risks, rendering the political contribution rule unnecessary. The Commission also is proposing to amend the rule under the Advisers Act pertaining to books and records consistent with the proposed rescission.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-09-23T01:15:09.498411+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/09/10/2026-18424/political-contributions-by-certain-investment-advisers", "external_id": "2026-18424", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Securities and Exchange Commission · Federal", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-09-10", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T00:56:37.337+00:00"}, {"id": "d9703443-c2ca-4c7c-acfc-3cbfa9db2b92", "raw": {"title": "Electronic Disclosure by Group Health Plans Under ERISA (Proposed)", "agency": "U.S. Department of Labor, Employee Benefits Security Administration", "summary": "Proposed rule would establish a safe harbor for group health plans to electronically disclose required documents to participants, requiring notice of internet availability and initial notice of default e-delivery rights.", "source_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/07/23/2026-14917/electronic-disclosure-by-group-health-plans-under-erisa", "jurisdiction": "Federal - United States", "effective_date": "", "published_date": "2026-02-25", "why_it_applies": "iX Health operates in the healthcare sector and handles health information, which may affect communication requirements with covered health plan members."}, "title": "Electronic Disclosure by Group Health Plans Under ERISA (Proposed)", "agency": "U.S. Department of Labor, Employee Benefits Security Administration", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "Proposed rule would establish a safe harbor for group health plans to electronically disclose required documents to participants, requiring notice of internet availability and initial notice of default e-delivery rights.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-02T21:57:57.310024+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/07/23/2026-14917/electronic-disclosure-by-group-health-plans-under-erisa", "external_id": "6c0572f0-c971-4219-aeb2-9ec37339079f:https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/07/23/2026-14917/electronic-disclosure-by-group-health-plans-under-erisa", "duplicate_of": "57aecc05-7dfc-454e-9692-c30d9f79cf92", "jurisdiction": "Federal - United States", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-02-25", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T00:56:37.15+00:00"}, {"id": "8b1b02ba-69d5-41db-ba23-6ec0a4d1ea5d", "raw": {"title": "Digital ATA carnet (eATA) acceptance for temporary admission", "agency": "UK Government / HMRC", "summary": "eATA is the digital version of the ATA carnet, used for temporary admission of goods such as exhibition equipment, samples, and professional tools; from 1 June 2026, the UK, EU, Norway, and Switzerland accept digital carnets presented via a smartphone app, and businesses that regularly move temporary goods across these borders should register for the digital service.", "source_url": "https://logisticsedge.co.uk/articles/digital-customs-compliance-uk-2026/", "jurisdiction": "United Kingdom", "effective_date": "2026-06-01", "published_date": ""}, "title": "Digital ATA carnet (eATA) acceptance for temporary admission", "agency": "UK Government / HMRC", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "eATA is the digital version of the ATA carnet, used for temporary admission of goods such as exhibition equipment, samples, and professional tools; from 1 June 2026, the UK, EU, Norway, and Switzerland accept digital carnets presented via a smartphone app, and businesses that regularly move temporary goods across these borders should register for the digital service.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-02T22:08:13.893016+00:00", "source_url": "https://logisticsedge.co.uk/articles/digital-customs-compliance-uk-2026/", "external_id": "51973b66-f862-464c-9a81-abdc54bbfd7c:https://logisticsedge.co.uk/articles/digital-customs-compliance-uk-2026/", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "United Kingdom", "effective_date": "2026-06-01", "published_date": null, "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T00:56:37.237+00:00"}, {"id": "e3bfc23c-7cf5-47fb-a847-e87aba151f97", "raw": {"title": "42 CFR Part 2 - Civil Enforcement Program for Substance Use Disorder Records", "agency": "U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Office for Civil Rights (OCR)", "summary": "Healthcare entities that handle substance use disorder treatment records must now comply with federal confidentiality requirements, with OCR actively accepting and enforcing complaints for violations.", "source_url": "https://omnimd.com/blog/healthcare-regulatory-compliance/", "jurisdiction": "Federal - United States", "effective_date": "2026-02-16", "published_date": "", "why_it_applies": "iX Health operates in the healthcare sector and handles health/patient information through revenue cycle operations that may involve processing claims related to substance use disorder treatment."}, "title": "42 CFR Part 2 - Civil Enforcement Program for Substance Use Disorder Records", "agency": "U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Office for Civil Rights (OCR)", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "Healthcare entities that handle substance use disorder treatment records must now comply with federal confidentiality requirements, with OCR actively accepting and enforcing complaints for violations.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-05T21:54:15.473427+00:00", "source_url": "https://omnimd.com/blog/healthcare-regulatory-compliance/", "external_id": "6c0572f0-c971-4219-aeb2-9ec37339079f:https://omnimd.com/blog/healthcare-regulatory-compliance/", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Federal - United States", "effective_date": "2026-02-16", "published_date": null, "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T00:56:37.237+00:00"}, {"id": "f7d6c397-e6f3-4ea1-9943-6ec0b3cdf128", "raw": {"title": "Amendments to Regulation S-P", "agency": "U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)", "summary": "Requires financial institutions including broker-dealers and investment advisers to implement written incident response programs for detecting, responding to, and remediating customer data breaches.", "source_url": "https://corsicatech.com/blog/sec-cybersecurity-compliance-update/", "jurisdiction": "Federal - United States", "effective_date": "2026-06-03", "published_date": ""}, "title": "Amendments to Regulation S-P", "agency": "U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "Requires financial institutions including broker-dealers and investment advisers to implement written incident response programs for detecting, responding to, and remediating customer data breaches.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-02T22:04:02.237588+00:00", "source_url": "https://corsicatech.com/blog/sec-cybersecurity-compliance-update/", "external_id": "898fcee3-a2ba-4bbc-9a47-ae7f1d9d5a14:https://corsicatech.com/blog/sec-cybersecurity-compliance-update/", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Federal - United States", "effective_date": "2026-06-03", "published_date": null, "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T00:56:37.237+00:00"}, {"id": "3d7551f4-27d2-459e-840c-5f8717d40300", "raw": {"type": "Proposed Rule", "title": "Interval Fund Modernization; Expansion of Multiple Share Class to Registered Closed-End Management Investment Companies and Business Development Companies", "pdf_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-10-05/pdf/2026-20360.pdf", "abstract": "The Securities and Exchange Commission (the \\"Commission\\") is proposing to amend the rule under the Investment Company Act of 1940 that allows registered closed-end management investment companies and business development companies (collectively, \\"regulated closed-end funds\\") to make repurchase offers to shareholders at net asset value (\\"NAV\\") at periodic intervals pursuant to a fundamental policy (\\"interval funds\\"). The proposed amendments would increase flexibility in the rule''s repurchase offer framework and modify the rule''s liquidity management requirements. The proposal is designed to modernize the framework applicable to these funds by allowing them to better match the liquidity profile of the assets in which they invest, while continuing to provide the operational infrastructure and investor protection of the Investment Company Act of 1940. We also propose amending certain rules that would permit regulated closed-end funds to issue multiple share classes, consistent with routine exemptive relief provided to these funds, and to require certain related disclosure in funds'' prospectuses. We further propose to require disclosures in all regulated closed-end fund shareholder reports, a legend in their prospectuses, and an increase in the dollar amount used for the prospectus expense example, to provide investors with information about fund expenses similar to that provided by registered open-end funds. As a result of these amendments for interval funds and multiple share class regulated closed-end funds, we propose to rescind existing related exemptive orders.", "agencies": [{"id": 466, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/securities-and-exchange-commission", "name": "Securities and Exchange Commission", "slug": "securities-and-exchange-commission", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/466", "raw_name": "SECURITIES AND EXCHANGE COMMISSION", "parent_id": null}], "excerpts": "The Securities and Exchange Commission (the \\"Commission\\") is proposing to amend the rule under the Investment Company Act of 1940 that allows registered closed-end management investment companies and business development companies (collectively,...", "html_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/10/05/2026-20360/interval-fund-modernization-expansion-of-multiple-share-class-to-registered-closed-end-management", "raw_text_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/full_text/text/2026/10/05/2026-20360.txt", "document_number": "2026-20360", "publication_date": "2026-10-05", "countries_from_full_text": false, "public_inspection_pdf_url": "https://public-inspection.federalregister.gov/2026-20360.pdf?1790945118"}, "title": "Interval Fund Modernization; Expansion of Multiple Share Class to Registered Closed-End Management Investment Companies and Business Development Companies", "agency": "Securities and Exchange Commission", "source": "federal_register", "summary": "The Securities and Exchange Commission (the \\"Commission\\") is proposing to amend the rule under the Investment Company Act of 1940 that allows registered closed-end management investment companies and business development companies (collectively, \\"regulated closed-end funds\\") to make repurchase offers to shareholders at net asset value (\\"NAV\\") at periodic intervals pursuant to a fundamental policy (\\"interval funds\\"). The proposed amendments would increase flexibility in the rule''s repurchase offer framework and modify the rule''s liquidity management requirements. The proposal is designed to modernize the framework applicable to these funds by allowing them to better match the liquidity profile of the assets in which they invest, while continuing to provide the operational infrastructure and investor protection of the Investment Company Act of 1940. We also propose amending certain rules that would permit regulated closed-end funds to issue multiple share classes, consistent with routine exemptive relief provided to these funds, and to require certain related disclosure in funds'' prospectuses. We further propose to require disclosures in all regulated closed-end fund shareholder reports, a legend in their prospectuses, and an increase in the dollar amount used for the prospectus expense example, to provide investors with information about fund expenses similar to that provided by registered open-end funds. As a result of these amendments for interval funds and multiple share class regulated closed-end funds, we propose to rescind existing related exemptive orders.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-03T11:00:06.406728+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/10/05/2026-20360/interval-fund-modernization-expansion-of-multiple-share-class-to-registered-closed-end-management", "external_id": "2026-20360", "duplicate_of": "09ee5069-f80d-4d96-b524-07b1afd02a2e", "jurisdiction": "Securities and Exchange Commission · Federal", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-10-05", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T01:15:37.747+00:00"}, {"id": "e6249c7b-82a4-4e10-a7ed-c45c246d4100", "raw": {"type": "Rule", "title": "Transparency in Coverage", "pdf_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-10-06/pdf/2026-20447.pdf", "abstract": "These final rules set forth requirements that amend the regulations under the Public Health Service Act, the Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974, and the Internal Revenue Code regarding price transparency reporting requirements for non- grandfathered group health plans and health insurance issuers offering non-grandfathered group and individual health insurance coverage. Specifically, these final rules are intended to improve the standardization, accuracy, and accessibility of public pricing disclosures in line with the goals of Executive Order 14221, \\"Making America Healthy Again by Empowering Patients With Clear, Accurate, and Actionable Healthcare Pricing Information.\\" With respect to the In- network Rate and out-of-network Allowed Amount machine-readable files, these final rules aim to achieve these goals by adding new contextual files and additional data elements like product type, provider network name, and provider network identifier; changing the reporting level for aggregation of data; removing in-network rates for unlikely provider- to-service mappings; increasing the reporting period and lowering the claims threshold for out-of-network historical data; and reducing the reporting cadence. These final rules also aim to improve the findability of all publicly disclosed machine-readable files required under the Transparency in Coverage rules, including the prescription drug file, by requiring a text file containing contact information for the files, and a footer with website URLs. These final rules also require pricing information that is made available through an online consumer tool and on paper (upon request), to also be made available by phone, and establish that the satisfaction of such requirement also satisfies the requirements of section 114 of the No Surprises Act (including for grandfathered group health plans and health insurance issuers offering grandfathered group and individual health insurance coverage that are not otherwise subject to these final rules).", "agencies": [{"id": 497, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/treasury-department", "name": "Treasury Department", "slug": "treasury-department", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/497", "raw_name": "DEPARTMENT OF THE TREASURY", "parent_id": null}, {"id": 254, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/internal-revenue-service", "name": "Internal Revenue Service", "slug": "internal-revenue-service", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/254", "raw_name": "Internal Revenue Service", "parent_id": 497}, {"id": 271, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/labor-department", "name": "Labor Department", "slug": "labor-department", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/271", "raw_name": "DEPARTMENT OF LABOR", "parent_id": null}, {"id": 131, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/employee-benefits-security-administration", "name": "Employee Benefits Security Administration", "slug": "employee-benefits-security-administration", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/131", "raw_name": "Employee Benefits Security Administration", "parent_id": 271}, {"id": 221, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/health-and-human-services-department", "name": "Health and Human Services Department", "slug": "health-and-human-services-department", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/221", "raw_name": "DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES", "parent_id": null}], "excerpts": "These final rules set forth requirements that amend the regulations under the Public Health Service Act, the Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974, and the Internal Revenue Code regarding price transparency reporting requirements for non-...", "html_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/10/06/2026-20447/transparency-in-coverage", "raw_text_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/full_text/text/2026/10/06/2026-20447.txt", "document_number": "2026-20447", "publication_date": "2026-10-06", "countries_from_full_text": false, "public_inspection_pdf_url": "https://public-inspection.federalregister.gov/2026-20447.pdf?1791204316"}, "title": "Transparency in Coverage", "agency": "Internal Revenue Service", "source": "federal_register", "summary": "These final rules set forth requirements that amend the regulations under the Public Health Service Act, the Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974, and the Internal Revenue Code regarding price transparency reporting requirements for non- grandfathered group health plans and health insurance issuers offering non-grandfathered group and individual health insurance coverage. Specifically, these final rules are intended to improve the standardization, accuracy, and accessibility of public pricing disclosures in line with the goals of Executive Order 14221, \\"Making America Healthy Again by Empowering Patients With Clear, Accurate, and Actionable Healthcare Pricing Information.\\" With respect to the In- network Rate and out-of-network Allowed Amount machine-readable files, these final rules aim to achieve these goals by adding new contextual files and additional data elements like product type, provider network name, and provider network identifier; changing the reporting level for aggregation of data; removing in-network rates for unlikely provider- to-service mappings; increasing the reporting period and lowering the claims threshold for out-of-network historical data; and reducing the reporting cadence. These final rules also aim to improve the findability of all publicly disclosed machine-readable files required under the Transparency in Coverage rules, including the prescription drug file, by requiring a text file containing contact information for the files, and a footer with website URLs. These final rules also require pricing information that is made available through an online consumer tool and on paper (upon request), to also be made available by phone, and establish that the satisfaction of such requirement also satisfies the requirements of section 114 of the No Surprises Act (including for grandfathered group health plans and health insurance issuers offering grandfathered group and individual health insurance coverage that are not otherwise subject to these final rules).", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-06T08:00:07.538021+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/10/06/2026-20447/transparency-in-coverage", "external_id": "2026-20447", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Internal Revenue Service · Federal", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-10-06", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": null}, {"id": "887f9647-771b-4941-8ed6-f50d3b99578d", "raw": {"feed": "https://www.fca.org.uk/publications/rss.xml"}, "title": "CP26/34: Preparing for the new transaction reporting regime", "agency": "Financial Conduct Authority (FCA)", "source": "fca_publications", "summary": "We’re consulting on guidance, consequential amendments and transitional provisions to help firms prepare for the new transaction reporting regime. ... Read CP26/34 (PDF) ... Why we are consultingWe are consulting on proposals to help firms implement our new transaction reporting rules.These include:&nbsp;Guidance focused on the changes we made in our new rules.&nbsp;Provisions to give firms clarity on the transition to the future transaction reporting regime.Amendments to our Handbook and Technical Standards to reference MAR 13, MAR 14 and MAR 15.A proposed approach to carrying across relevant", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-06T21:00:20.484827+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.fca.org.uk/publications/consultation-papers/cp26-34-preparing-new-transaction-reporting-regime", "external_id": "/publications/consultation-papers/cp26-34-preparing-new-transaction-reporting-regime", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "United Kingdom", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-10-02", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T00:56:37.15+00:00"}, {"id": "fbd71f39-9908-43f1-97b0-b25014d45630", "raw": {"title": "ITAR Review of U.S. Munitions List - Omnibus Proposed Changes (Proposed Rule)", "agency": "U.S. Department of State, Directorate of Defense Trade Controls (DDTC)", "summary": "This proposed rule removes various items from ITAR controls, including specific types of items and broader catch-all controls, and creates a new exemption from ITAR licensing requirements for temporary export of a foreign defense article to its foreign manufacturer.", "source_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/10/01/2026-20079/international-traffic-in-arms-regulations-itar-review-of-the-us-munitions-list-and-related", "jurisdiction": "United States", "effective_date": "", "published_date": "2026-10-01"}, "title": "ITAR Review of U.S. Munitions List - Omnibus Proposed Changes (Proposed Rule)", "agency": "U.S. Department of State, Directorate of Defense Trade Controls (DDTC)", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "This proposed rule removes various items from ITAR controls, including specific types of items and broader catch-all controls, and creates a new exemption from ITAR licensing requirements for temporary export of a foreign defense article to its foreign manufacturer.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-04T04:31:44.269229+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/10/01/2026-20079/international-traffic-in-arms-regulations-itar-review-of-the-us-munitions-list-and-related", "external_id": "9f3948e7-032e-4e70-8413-6fe598d9168b:https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/10/01/2026-20079/international-traffic-in-arms-regulations-itar-review-of-the-us-munitions-list-and-related", "duplicate_of": "99270832-5da0-4be0-bd8c-6e390c9aa776", "jurisdiction": "United States", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-10-01", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T00:56:37.237+00:00"}, {"id": "fd6a5290-d3f5-4035-bacb-dcf404ae275d", "raw": {"title": "GB/T 47393-2026 Technical Guidelines for Waste Lithium Battery Recycling", "agency": "National Standardization Administration of China (SAC)", "summary": "Establishes comprehensive technical protocols for handling and chemical recycling of spent lithium-ion batteries with high purity standards for recovered materials.", "source_url": "https://www.china-certification.com/en/new-technical-guidelines-for-waste-lithium-battery-recycling-set-new-standards-in-china/", "jurisdiction": "China", "effective_date": "", "published_date": "", "why_it_applies": "Rheintal Batterie GmbH (test) is a battery manufacturing company, and this regulation establishes technical protocols for battery recycling operations."}, "title": "GB/T 47393-2026 Technical Guidelines for Waste Lithium Battery Recycling", "agency": "National Standardization Administration of China (SAC)", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "Establishes comprehensive technical protocols for handling and chemical recycling of spent lithium-ion batteries with high purity standards for recovered materials.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-08T22:58:59.270292+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.china-certification.com/en/new-technical-guidelines-for-waste-lithium-battery-recycling-set-new-standards-in-china/", "external_id": "1ae65692-f23c-4109-8064-72a34d27f2be:https://www.china-certification.com/en/new-technical-guidelines-for-waste-lithium-battery-recycling-set-new-standards-in-china/", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "China", "effective_date": null, "published_date": null, "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T23:08:02.497+00:00"}, {"id": "7909bbc1-5fbd-4220-997b-633802049320", "raw": {"type": "Rule", "title": "Guide To Minimize Biological Hazards in Ready-to-Eat Fresh-Cut Produce; Guidance for Industry; Availability", "pdf_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-08-12/pdf/2026-16420.pdf", "abstract": "The Food and Drug Administration (FDA or we) is announcing the availability of a final guidance for industry entitled \\"Guide to Minimize Biological Hazards in Ready-to-Eat Fresh-Cut Produce.\\" This guidance supersedes a previous guidance, entitled \\"Guide to Minimize Microbial Food Safety Hazards of Fresh-Cut Fruits and Vegetables,\\" issued in 2008, and is a final guidance to the draft guidance for industry entitled \\"Guide to Minimize Food Safety Hazards of Fresh-Cut Produce\\" issued in 2018. The guidance is intended to help manufacturers or processors of ready-to-eat fresh-cut produce that is not a low-moisture food comply with applicable requirements in our regulations on current good manufacturing practices for hazard analysis and risk-based preventive controls for human food.", "agencies": [{"id": 221, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/health-and-human-services-department", "name": "Health and Human Services Department", "slug": "health-and-human-services-department", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/221", "raw_name": "DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES", "parent_id": null}, {"id": 199, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/food-and-drug-administration", "name": "Food and Drug Administration", "slug": "food-and-drug-administration", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/199", "raw_name": "Food and Drug Administration", "parent_id": 221}], "excerpts": "The Food and Drug Administration (FDA or we) is announcing the availability of a final guidance for industry entitled \\"Guide to Minimize Biological Hazards in Ready-to-Eat Fresh-Cut Produce.\\" This guidance supersedes a previous guidance, entitled...", "html_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/08/12/2026-16420/guide-to-minimize-biological-hazards-in-ready-to-eat-fresh-cut-produce-guidance-for-industry", "raw_text_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/full_text/text/2026/08/12/2026-16420.txt", "document_number": "2026-16420", "publication_date": "2026-08-12", "countries_from_full_text": false, "public_inspection_pdf_url": "https://public-inspection.federalregister.gov/2026-16420.pdf?1786452316"}, "title": "Guide To Minimize Biological Hazards in Ready-to-Eat Fresh-Cut Produce; Guidance for Industry; Availability", "agency": "Food and Drug Administration", "source": "federal_register", "summary": "The Food and Drug Administration (FDA or we) is announcing the availability of a final guidance for industry entitled \\"Guide to Minimize Biological Hazards in Ready-to-Eat Fresh-Cut Produce.\\" This guidance supersedes a previous guidance, entitled \\"Guide to Minimize Microbial Food Safety Hazards of Fresh-Cut Fruits and Vegetables,\\" issued in 2008, and is a final guidance to the draft guidance for industry entitled \\"Guide to Minimize Food Safety Hazards of Fresh-Cut Produce\\" issued in 2018. The guidance is intended to help manufacturers or processors of ready-to-eat fresh-cut produce that is not a low-moisture food comply with applicable requirements in our regulations on current good manufacturing practices for hazard analysis and risk-based preventive controls for human food.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-09-23T01:15:09.498411+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/08/12/2026-16420/guide-to-minimize-biological-hazards-in-ready-to-eat-fresh-cut-produce-guidance-for-industry", "external_id": "2026-16420", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Food and Drug Administration · Federal", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-08-12", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": null}, {"id": "9652512c-ad5d-4575-aaa8-7e319ff96007", "raw": {"title": "Anti-Monopoly Review Special Action for Lithium Battery Industry", "agency": "State Administration for Market Regulation (SAMR)", "summary": "Special governance campaign targeting lithium battery sector mergers and acquisitions to prevent involution competition and requiring anti-monopoly concentration review.", "source_url": "https://www.fywpower.com/news/china-tightens-lithium-battery-industry-regulation-precision-governance-to-end-involution-competitio-390703.html", "jurisdiction": "China", "effective_date": "", "published_date": "2026-08-21", "why_it_applies": "Rheintal Batterie GmbH (test) is a battery manufacturing company, and this regulation targets the lithium battery sector for anti-monopoly review."}, "title": "Anti-Monopoly Review Special Action for Lithium Battery Industry", "agency": "State Administration for Market Regulation (SAMR)", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "Special governance campaign targeting lithium battery sector mergers and acquisitions to prevent involution competition and requiring anti-monopoly concentration review.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-08T22:58:59.951719+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.fywpower.com/news/china-tightens-lithium-battery-industry-regulation-precision-governance-to-end-involution-competitio-390703.html", "external_id": "1ae65692-f23c-4109-8064-72a34d27f2be:https://www.fywpower.com/news/china-tightens-lithium-battery-industry-regulation-precision-governance-to-end-involution-competitio-390703.html", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "China", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-08-21", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T23:08:02.497+00:00"}, {"id": "459d3a24-8781-4eb4-8d0e-29f4fe192b45", "raw": {"type": "Notice", "title": "Notice of Conforming Amendments to Product Exclusions: China''s Acts, Policies, and Practices Related to Technology Transfer, Intellectual Property, and Innovation", "pdf_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-09-02/pdf/2026-17925.pdf", "abstract": "Effective July 1, 2026, the U.S. International Trade Commission (USITC) implemented certain changes to statistical reporting categories in the Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS). As a result of these changes, USTR is making conforming amendments to four product exclusions associated with the Section 301 investigation of China Acts, Policies, and Practices Related to Technology Transfer, Intellectual Property, and Innovation.", "agencies": [{"id": 491, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/trade-representative-office-of-united-states", "name": "Trade Representative, Office of United States", "slug": "trade-representative-office-of-united-states", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/491", "raw_name": "OFFICE OF THE UNITED STATES TRADE REPRESENTATIVE", "parent_id": null}], "excerpts": "Effective July 1, 2026, the U.S. International Trade Commission (USITC) implemented certain changes to statistical reporting categories in the Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS). As a result of these changes, USTR is making...", "html_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/09/02/2026-17925/notice-of-conforming-amendments-to-product-exclusions-chinas-acts-policies-and-practices-related-to", "raw_text_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/full_text/text/2026/09/02/2026-17925.txt", "document_number": "2026-17925", "publication_date": "2026-09-02", "countries_from_full_text": false, "public_inspection_pdf_url": "https://public-inspection.federalregister.gov/2026-17925.pdf?1788266709"}, "title": "Notice of Conforming Amendments to Product Exclusions: China''s Acts, Policies, and Practices Related to Technology Transfer, Intellectual Property, and Innovation", "agency": "Trade Representative, Office of United States", "source": "federal_register", "summary": "Effective July 1, 2026, the U.S. International Trade Commission (USITC) implemented certain changes to statistical reporting categories in the Harmonized Tariff Schedule of the United States (HTSUS). As a result of these changes, USTR is making conforming amendments to four product exclusions associated with the Section 301 investigation of China Acts, Policies, and Practices Related to Technology Transfer, Intellectual Property, and Innovation.", "category": "geopolitical", "countries": ["China"], "created_at": "2026-10-04T02:29:09.161622+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/09/02/2026-17925/notice-of-conforming-amendments-to-product-exclusions-chinas-acts-policies-and-practices-related-to", "external_id": "2026-17925", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Trade Representative, Office of United States · Federal", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-09-02", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": null}, {"id": "bba5816e-7ce6-42cb-9ffc-b264aad5ef3d", "raw": {"type": "Rule", "title": "Medicaid Program; Prohibition on Federal Medicaid and Children''s Health Insurance Program Funding for Sex-Rejecting Procedures Furnished to Children", "pdf_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-08-13/pdf/2026-16508.pdf", "abstract": "This final rule requires that a State Medicaid plan must provide that the Medicaid agency will not make payment under the plan for sex-rejecting procedures for children under 18, and prohibits the use of Federal Medicaid dollars to fund sex-rejecting procedures for individuals under the age of 18. In addition, this final rule requires that a separate State Children''s Health Insurance Program (CHIP) plan must provide that the CHIP agency will not make payment under the plan for sex-rejecting procedures for children under 19, and prohibits the use of Federal CHIP dollars to fund sex-rejecting procedures for individuals under the age of 19. For Medicaid and CHIP beneficiaries who are actively receiving cross-sex hormone therapy, State Medicaid and CHIP agencies may continue to claim Federal Financial Participation for those hormone therapy medications for a period of up to 6 months from the effective date of this final rule.", "agencies": [{"id": 221, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/health-and-human-services-department", "name": "Health and Human Services Department", "slug": "health-and-human-services-department", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/221", "raw_name": "DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES", "parent_id": null}, {"id": 45, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/centers-for-medicare-medicaid-services", "name": "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services", "slug": "centers-for-medicare-medicaid-services", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/45", "raw_name": "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services", "parent_id": 221}], "excerpts": "This final rule requires that a State Medicaid plan must provide that the Medicaid agency will not make payment under the plan for sex-rejecting procedures for children under 18, and prohibits the use of Federal Medicaid dollars to fund sex-rejecting...", "html_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/08/13/2026-16508/medicaid-program-prohibition-on-federal-medicaid-and-childrens-health-insurance-program-funding-for", "raw_text_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/full_text/text/2026/08/13/2026-16508.txt", "document_number": "2026-16508", "publication_date": "2026-08-13", "countries_from_full_text": false, "public_inspection_pdf_url": "https://public-inspection.federalregister.gov/2026-16508.pdf?1786479307"}, "title": "Medicaid Program; Prohibition on Federal Medicaid and Children''s Health Insurance Program Funding for Sex-Rejecting Procedures Furnished to Children", "agency": "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services", "source": "federal_register", "summary": "This final rule requires that a State Medicaid plan must provide that the Medicaid agency will not make payment under the plan for sex-rejecting procedures for children under 18, and prohibits the use of Federal Medicaid dollars to fund sex-rejecting procedures for individuals under the age of 18. In addition, this final rule requires that a separate State Children''s Health Insurance Program (CHIP) plan must provide that the CHIP agency will not make payment under the plan for sex-rejecting procedures for children under 19, and prohibits the use of Federal CHIP dollars to fund sex-rejecting procedures for individuals under the age of 19. For Medicaid and CHIP beneficiaries who are actively receiving cross-sex hormone therapy, State Medicaid and CHIP agencies may continue to claim Federal Financial Participation for those hormone therapy medications for a period of up to 6 months from the effective date of this final rule.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-09-23T01:15:09.498411+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/08/13/2026-16508/medicaid-program-prohibition-on-federal-medicaid-and-childrens-health-insurance-program-funding-for", "external_id": "2026-16508", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services · Federal", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-08-13", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": null}, {"id": "b57cc23d-7d69-4507-a347-db12bf907675", "raw": {"title": "Digital Product Passport Registry Operational Launch", "agency": "European Commission", "summary": "The DPP Registry became operational, enabling manufacturers to begin technical preparations for mandatory battery passport compliance starting February 2027.", "source_url": "https://single-market-economy.ec.europa.eu/single-market/digital-product-passport/batteries_en", "jurisdiction": "European Union", "effective_date": "", "published_date": "2026-07-20", "why_it_applies": "Rheintal Batterie GmbH (test) is a battery manufacturing company, and this operational launch enables technical preparations for mandatory battery passport compliance."}, "title": "Digital Product Passport Registry Operational Launch", "agency": "European Commission", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "The DPP Registry became operational, enabling manufacturers to begin technical preparations for mandatory battery passport compliance starting February 2027.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-08T22:58:58.998108+00:00", "source_url": "https://single-market-economy.ec.europa.eu/single-market/digital-product-passport/batteries_en", "external_id": "1ae65692-f23c-4109-8064-72a34d27f2be:https://single-market-economy.ec.europa.eu/single-market/digital-product-passport/batteries_en", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "European Union", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-07-20", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T23:08:02.497+00:00"}, {"id": "60b6d317-a535-4602-a4e5-82d8db89d949", "raw": {"title": "Wisconsin Advanced Practice Registered Nurse License Transition", "agency": "Wisconsin Department of Safety and Professional Services", "summary": "Wisconsin is transitioning Advanced Practice Nurse Prescriber credentials to Advanced Practice Registered Nurse licenses beginning September 1, 2026.", "source_url": "https://verisys.com/resources/july-2026-healthcare-compliance-updates/", "jurisdiction": "State of Wisconsin", "effective_date": "2026-09-01", "published_date": ""}, "title": "Wisconsin Advanced Practice Registered Nurse License Transition", "agency": "Wisconsin Department of Safety and Professional Services", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "Wisconsin is transitioning Advanced Practice Nurse Prescriber credentials to Advanced Practice Registered Nurse licenses beginning September 1, 2026.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-02T21:59:41.064956+00:00", "source_url": "https://verisys.com/resources/july-2026-healthcare-compliance-updates/", "external_id": "6c0572f0-c971-4219-aeb2-9ec37339079f:https://verisys.com/resources/july-2026-healthcare-compliance-updates/", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "State of Wisconsin", "effective_date": "2026-09-01", "published_date": null, "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T00:56:37.237+00:00"}, {"id": "1c9e00c1-2aaa-4310-ad75-08d5161876a8", "raw": {"type": "Rule", "title": "Beneficial Ownership Information Reporting Requirement Revision", "pdf_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-08-14/pdf/2026-16576.pdf", "abstract": "FinCEN is issuing this final rule to adopt as final and with certain limited changes the interim final rule issued on March 26, 2025, which narrowed beneficial ownership information (BOI) reporting requirements under FinCEN''s regulations implementing the Corporate Transparency Act (CTA). In particular, this final rule not only continues to exempt reporting companies from having to report the BOI of U.S. person beneficial owners and U.S. person beneficial owners from having to provide BOI to reporting companies; it also exempts reporting companies from having to submit information about their U.S. person company applicants to FinCEN and exempts U.S. person company applicants from any obligation to provide their information. In addition, the final rule exempts all U.S. persons from the requirement to update information already provided to FinCEN in connection with obtaining a FinCEN identifier (FinCEN ID).", "agencies": [{"id": 497, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/treasury-department", "name": "Treasury Department", "slug": "treasury-department", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/497", "raw_name": "DEPARTMENT OF THE TREASURY", "parent_id": null}, {"id": 194, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/financial-crimes-enforcement-network", "name": "Financial Crimes Enforcement Network", "slug": "financial-crimes-enforcement-network", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/194", "raw_name": "Financial Crimes Enforcement Network", "parent_id": 497}], "excerpts": "FinCEN is issuing this final rule to adopt as final and with certain limited changes the interim final rule issued on March 26, 2025, which narrowed beneficial ownership information (BOI) reporting requirements under FinCEN''s regulations implementing...", "html_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/08/14/2026-16576/beneficial-ownership-information-reporting-requirement-revision", "raw_text_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/full_text/text/2026/08/14/2026-16576.txt", "document_number": "2026-16576", "publication_date": "2026-08-14", "countries_from_full_text": false, "public_inspection_pdf_url": "https://public-inspection.federalregister.gov/2026-16576.pdf?1786625110"}, "title": "Beneficial Ownership Information Reporting Requirement Revision", "agency": "Financial Crimes Enforcement Network", "source": "federal_register", "summary": "FinCEN is issuing this final rule to adopt as final and with certain limited changes the interim final rule issued on March 26, 2025, which narrowed beneficial ownership information (BOI) reporting requirements under FinCEN''s regulations implementing the Corporate Transparency Act (CTA). In particular, this final rule not only continues to exempt reporting companies from having to report the BOI of U.S. person beneficial owners and U.S. person beneficial owners from having to provide BOI to reporting companies; it also exempts reporting companies from having to submit information about their U.S. person company applicants to FinCEN and exempts U.S. person company applicants from any obligation to provide their information. In addition, the final rule exempts all U.S. persons from the requirement to update information already provided to FinCEN in connection with obtaining a FinCEN identifier (FinCEN ID).", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-09-23T01:20:05.967225+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/08/14/2026-16576/beneficial-ownership-information-reporting-requirement-revision", "external_id": "2026-16576", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Financial Crimes Enforcement Network · Federal", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-08-14", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": null}, {"id": "a94f4e81-33a8-4410-b1f9-a4d267a1e708", "raw": {"title": "Final Rule Amending Bank Secrecy Act Regulations", "agency": "Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), Department of the Treasury", "summary": "Exempts all U.S. persons from the requirement to update information already provided to FinCEN in connection with obtaining a FinCEN identifier.", "source_url": "https://www.federalreserve.gov/frrs/federal-reserve-regulatory-service.htm", "jurisdiction": "United States (Federal)", "effective_date": "2026-08-14", "published_date": "", "why_it_applies": "Meridian Pay (test) is a regulated financial services company subject to BSA/AML requirements."}, "title": "Final Rule Amending Bank Secrecy Act Regulations", "agency": "Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), Department of the Treasury", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "Exempts all U.S. persons from the requirement to update information already provided to FinCEN in connection with obtaining a FinCEN identifier.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-08T22:58:43.518908+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.federalreserve.gov/frrs/federal-reserve-regulatory-service.htm", "external_id": "cf926d2b-b1be-4d37-831c-7fbcd99edb02:https://www.federalreserve.gov/frrs/federal-reserve-regulatory-service.htm", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "United States (Federal)", "effective_date": "2026-08-14", "published_date": null, "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T23:08:02.497+00:00"}, {"id": "b9e8c7fa-ad74-4b14-82b0-9de152ac93f9", "raw": {"type": "Rule", "title": "Streamlining Export Controls for Drone Exports", "pdf_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-08-14/pdf/2026-16628.pdf", "abstract": "The Bureau of Industry and Security (BIS) is easing export controls on certain Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs or drones) and related parts, components, accessories, attachments, technology, and software under the Export Administration Regulations (EAR). Specifically, this rule: eliminates wind gust tolerance as a parameter for determining UAV controls under the EAR; increases the threshold for national security controls on certain UAVs from an endurance of 30 minutes to an endurance of 3 hours; makes conforming changes to remove national security controls on software and technology associated with UAVs with an endurance less than 3 hours; maintains military end-use and end-user controls on those lower endurance drones and associated software and technology; clarifies Commerce Control List (CCL) controls for certain UAVs specially designed for military use; and removes national security controls on certain specially designed parts, components, accessories, and attachments for such UAVs as they do not provide any significant military or intelligence capabilities.", "agencies": [{"id": 54, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/commerce-department", "name": "Commerce Department", "slug": "commerce-department", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/54", "raw_name": "DEPARTMENT OF COMMERCE", "parent_id": null}, {"id": 241, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/industry-and-security-bureau", "name": "Industry and Security Bureau", "slug": "industry-and-security-bureau", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/241", "raw_name": "Bureau of Industry and Security", "parent_id": 54}], "excerpts": "The Bureau of Industry and Security (BIS) is easing export controls on certain Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs or drones) and related parts, components, accessories, attachments, technology, and software under the Export Administration Regulations...", "html_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/08/14/2026-16628/streamlining-export-controls-for-drone-exports", "raw_text_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/full_text/text/2026/08/14/2026-16628.txt", "document_number": "2026-16628", "publication_date": "2026-08-14", "countries_from_full_text": false, "public_inspection_pdf_url": "https://public-inspection.federalregister.gov/2026-16628.pdf?1786625117"}, "title": "Streamlining Export Controls for Drone Exports", "agency": "Industry and Security Bureau", "source": "federal_register", "summary": "The Bureau of Industry and Security (BIS) is easing export controls on certain Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs or drones) and related parts, components, accessories, attachments, technology, and software under the Export Administration Regulations (EAR). Specifically, this rule: eliminates wind gust tolerance as a parameter for determining UAV controls under the EAR; increases the threshold for national security controls on certain UAVs from an endurance of 30 minutes to an endurance of 3 hours; makes conforming changes to remove national security controls on software and technology associated with UAVs with an endurance less than 3 hours; maintains military end-use and end-user controls on those lower endurance drones and associated software and technology; clarifies Commerce Control List (CCL) controls for certain UAVs specially designed for military use; and removes national security controls on certain specially designed parts, components, accessories, and attachments for such UAVs as they do not provide any significant military or intelligence capabilities.", "category": "geopolitical", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-04T02:29:09.161622+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/08/14/2026-16628/streamlining-export-controls-for-drone-exports", "external_id": "2026-16628", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Industry and Security Bureau · Federal", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-08-14", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": null}, {"id": "084d5690-893e-442d-bd74-76c26d03cf66", "raw": {"narrative_text": "Litigation Release No. 26632 / September 4, 2026 Securities and Exchange Commission v. Institutional Shareholder Services, Inc. , No. 2:26-mc-00078 (E.D. Pa. filed Sept. 4, 2026) SEC Files Subpoena Enforcement Action Against Institutional Shareholder Services Inc. On September 4, 2026, the Securities and Exchange Commission filed a subpoena enforcement action in the U.S. District Court for the Eastern District of Pennsylvania seeking an order to compel Institutional Shareholder Services, Inc. (“ISS”), an investment adviser registered with the SEC, to comply with an outstanding SEC administrative subpoena. According to the SEC’s application and supporting papers, ISS first refused to comply with routine requests from agency examinations staff and continues to refuse to fully comply with an administrative subpoena issued on July 21, 2026, by the Commission, ISS’s primary regulator. According to the SEC’s filing, the subpoena seeks documents highly relevant to its investigation of ISS’s compliance with the federal securities laws. ISS’s refusal to produce the required records to staff from the Division of Examinations, and now the Division of Enforcement, has impeded the Commission from advancing its mission and satisfying its statutory oversight and investor protection obligations, according to the filing. The SEC’s Division of Examinations routinely obtains client information during examinations to ensure that registrants are complying with the federal securities laws, including determining whether investment advisers are acting in the best interest of their clients. As described in the SEC’s application, in March 2026, the SEC’s Division of Examinations began conducting an examination of ISS and requested data relating to ISS’s proxy recommendations and votes—the core of ISS’s business—that would enable the SEC to evaluate ISS’s compliance with the federal securities laws. However, according to the SEC’s application, ISS failed to produce the requested information in its possession, custody, and control, including after the SEC’s Division of Enforcement opened an inquiry into ISS’s failure to produce the information requested by the examinations team and issued a narrowly tailored administrative subpoena. The SEC’s application further states that more than four months have elapsed since the examinations team’s original request, and despite extended deadlines and repeated efforts by the staff to reach a resolution, ISS has continued to refuse to produce all of the requested materials. The SEC’s filing requests that the Court enter an order compelling ISS to comply with the administrative subpoena. The SEC is continuing its fact-finding investigation and, to date, has not concluded that any individual or entity violated the federal securities laws. Resources SEC Memorandum of Law Return to top SEC homepage About the SEC Budget & Performance Careers Commission Votes Contact Contracts Transparency Accessibility & Disability Artificial Intelligence FOIA Inspector General No FEAR Act Data Ombuds SEC Excess Personal Property Guidance Websites Investor.gov Related Sites USA.gov Site Information Plain Writing Privacy & Security Site Map Stay connected. Sign up for email updates. Your email address Sign Up X Facebook Instagram RSS YouTube Email Updates", "release_number": "LR-26632"}, "title": "Institutional Shareholder Services, Inc.", "agency": "Securities and Exchange Commission", "source": "sec_enforcement", "summary": "SEC Files Subpoena Enforcement Action Against Institutional Shareholder Services Inc.", "category": "enforcement", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-09-30T02:59:36.125027+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/enforcement-litigation/litigation-releases/lr-26632", "external_id": "LR-26632", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Federal", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-09-04", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": null}, {"id": "99270832-5da0-4be0-bd8c-6e390c9aa776", "raw": {"type": "Proposed Rule", "title": "International Traffic in Arms Regulations (ITAR): Review of the U.S. Munitions List and Related Definitions and License Exemptions", "pdf_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-10-01/pdf/2026-20079.pdf", "abstract": "The Department of State (the Department) proposes to amend the International Traffic in Arms Regulations (ITAR) to ensure that the U.S. Munitions List (USML) focuses ITAR controls on the most sensitive technologies, to improve regulatory transparency and clarity, and to reduce regulatory burdens. The revisions proposed in this rule would remove certain items from the USML, update definitions and standardize the regulatory text, and add a new exemption to the licensing requirements of the ITAR. The Department also requests public comments to further refine ITAR controls.", "agencies": [{"id": 476, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/state-department", "name": "State Department", "slug": "state-department", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/476", "raw_name": "DEPARTMENT OF STATE", "parent_id": null}], "excerpts": "The Department of State (the Department) proposes to amend the International Traffic in Arms Regulations (ITAR) to ensure that the U.S. Munitions List (USML) focuses ITAR controls on the most sensitive technologies, to improve regulatory transparency...", "html_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/10/01/2026-20079/international-traffic-in-arms-regulations-itar-review-of-the-us-munitions-list-and-related", "raw_text_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/full_text/text/2026/10/01/2026-20079.txt", "document_number": "2026-20079", "publication_date": "2026-10-01", "countries_from_full_text": false, "public_inspection_pdf_url": "https://public-inspection.federalregister.gov/2026-20079.pdf?1790772310"}, "title": "International Traffic in Arms Regulations (ITAR): Review of the U.S. Munitions List and Related Definitions and License Exemptions", "agency": "State Department", "source": "federal_register", "summary": "The Department of State (the Department) proposes to amend the International Traffic in Arms Regulations (ITAR) to ensure that the U.S. Munitions List (USML) focuses ITAR controls on the most sensitive technologies, to improve regulatory transparency and clarity, and to reduce regulatory burdens. The revisions proposed in this rule would remove certain items from the USML, update definitions and standardize the regulatory text, and add a new exemption to the licensing requirements of the ITAR. The Department also requests public comments to further refine ITAR controls.", "category": "geopolitical", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-07T22:26:26.692973+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/10/01/2026-20079/international-traffic-in-arms-regulations-itar-review-of-the-us-munitions-list-and-related", "external_id": "2026-20079", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "State Department · Federal", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-10-01", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": null}, {"id": "b3b4e9c5-31fa-4a44-b01f-2d2fd42c6736", "raw": {"title": "Information Commission Structural Reform - Came into Effect", "agency": "UK Government / Information Commissioner''s Office", "summary": "The Information Commission officially replaced the Information Commissioner''s Office on 30 September 2026, following reforms introduced by the Data (Use and Access) Act 2025.", "source_url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/information-commission-succeeds-the-ico-as-uks-data-protection-regulator", "jurisdiction": "United Kingdom", "effective_date": "2026-09-30", "published_date": "", "why_it_applies": "Brightwell Wealth (test) handles personal consumer data and is subject to data protection regulatory oversight."}, "title": "Information Commission Structural Reform - Came into Effect", "agency": "UK Government / Information Commissioner''s Office", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "The Information Commission officially replaced the Information Commissioner''s Office on 30 September 2026, following reforms introduced by the Data (Use and Access) Act 2025.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-08T22:57:41.861726+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/information-commission-succeeds-the-ico-as-uks-data-protection-regulator", "external_id": "dd89ba7b-dc41-4b5b-8c7a-4507bc58f69c:https://www.gov.uk/government/news/information-commission-succeeds-the-ico-as-uks-data-protection-regulator", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "United Kingdom", "effective_date": "2026-09-30", "published_date": null, "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T23:08:02.497+00:00"}, {"id": "e41cd7e8-0231-4cd1-9ea3-ddc1e22b5792", "raw": {"type": "Proposed Rule", "title": "Adviser and Regulated Fund Custody Rules; Crypto Custody Rules", "pdf_url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2026-10-06/pdf/2026-20466.pdf", "abstract": "The Securities and Exchange Commission (the \\"Commission\\" or the \\"SEC\\") is proposing new custody rules under the Investment Company Act of 1940 (the \\"Investment Company Act\\") and amendments to related reporting and recordkeeping requirements to address how regulated investment companies may custody crypto securities and similar investments, and amendments to the custody rule and related reporting and recordkeeping rules under the Investment Advisers Act of 1940 (the \\"Advisers Act\\") to address how registered investment advisers may custody client crypto funds and securities. We are also proposing to amend the current custody rules to modernize their requirements, to better address current industry practices and feedback, and to implement certain conforming amendments. We are also proposing amendments to the recordkeeping rules under the Investment Company Act and Advisers Act related to these proposed modernization amendments to the custody rules. Additionally, we are proposing to redesignate the custody rule under the Advisers Act and to make corresponding amendments to Form ADV and Form ADV-E for registered investment advisers to reflect the Advisers Act custody rule redesignation. We are also proposing amendments to Form ADV for registered investment advisers and Form N-CEN for regulated investment companies and to improve the quality of public disclosures made on these forms. We are also proposing to add new questions to Form ADV and Form N-CEN related to tokenized private funds and regulated investment company shares, respectively.", "agencies": [{"id": 466, "url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/agencies/securities-and-exchange-commission", "name": "Securities and Exchange Commission", "slug": "securities-and-exchange-commission", "json_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/api/v1/agencies/466", "raw_name": "SECURITIES AND EXCHANGE COMMISSION", "parent_id": null}], "excerpts": "The Securities and Exchange Commission (the \\"Commission\\" or the \\"SEC\\") is proposing new custody rules under the Investment Company Act of 1940 (the \\"Investment Company Act\\") and amendments to related reporting and recordkeeping requirements to address...", "html_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/10/06/2026-20466/adviser-and-regulated-fund-custody-rules-crypto-custody-rules", "raw_text_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/full_text/text/2026/10/06/2026-20466.txt", "document_number": "2026-20466", "publication_date": "2026-10-06", "countries_from_full_text": false, "public_inspection_pdf_url": "https://public-inspection.federalregister.gov/2026-20466.pdf?1791204319"}, "title": "Adviser and Regulated Fund Custody Rules; Crypto Custody Rules", "agency": "Securities and Exchange Commission", "source": "federal_register", "summary": "The Securities and Exchange Commission (the \\"Commission\\" or the \\"SEC\\") is proposing new custody rules under the Investment Company Act of 1940 (the \\"Investment Company Act\\") and amendments to related reporting and recordkeeping requirements to address how regulated investment companies may custody crypto securities and similar investments, and amendments to the custody rule and related reporting and recordkeeping rules under the Investment Advisers Act of 1940 (the \\"Advisers Act\\") to address how registered investment advisers may custody client crypto funds and securities. We are also proposing to amend the current custody rules to modernize their requirements, to better address current industry practices and feedback, and to implement certain conforming amendments. We are also proposing amendments to the recordkeeping rules under the Investment Company Act and Advisers Act related to these proposed modernization amendments to the custody rules. Additionally, we are proposing to redesignate the custody rule under the Advisers Act and to make corresponding amendments to Form ADV and Form ADV-E for registered investment advisers to reflect the Advisers Act custody rule redesignation. We are also proposing amendments to Form ADV for registered investment advisers and Form N-CEN for regulated investment companies and to improve the quality of public disclosures made on these forms. We are also proposing to add new questions to Form ADV and Form N-CEN related to tokenized private funds and regulated investment company shares, respectively.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-06T08:00:07.538021+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/10/06/2026-20466/adviser-and-regulated-fund-custody-rules-crypto-custody-rules", "external_id": "2026-20466", "duplicate_of": "b12d3b2c-d9a5-40a8-89a8-2c7981cdf93b", "jurisdiction": "Securities and Exchange Commission · Federal", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-10-06", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T01:15:37.747+00:00"}, {"id": "3e732ef2-d828-46bf-8208-9fd45dc43b28", "raw": {"title": "HIPAA Notice of Privacy Practices Updates - 42 CFR Part 2 Alignment", "agency": "U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS), Office for Civil Rights (OCR)", "summary": "HIPAA-covered entities must comply with new federal requirements that change how substance use disorder treatment records are addressed in privacy documentation, revising the HIPAA Privacy Rule to better align with the Federal Confidentiality of Substance Use Disorder Patient Records regulations.", "source_url": "https://www.gammacompliance.com/compliance101/hipaa-update-sud-records-2026", "jurisdiction": "Federal (United States)", "effective_date": "2026-02-16", "published_date": ""}, "title": "HIPAA Notice of Privacy Practices Updates - 42 CFR Part 2 Alignment", "agency": "U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS), Office for Civil Rights (OCR)", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "HIPAA-covered entities must comply with new federal requirements that change how substance use disorder treatment records are addressed in privacy documentation, revising the HIPAA Privacy Rule to better align with the Federal Confidentiality of Substance Use Disorder Patient Records regulations.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-02T21:59:39.470039+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.gammacompliance.com/compliance101/hipaa-update-sud-records-2026", "external_id": "6c0572f0-c971-4219-aeb2-9ec37339079f:https://www.gammacompliance.com/compliance101/hipaa-update-sud-records-2026", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Federal (United States)", "effective_date": "2026-02-16", "published_date": null, "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T00:56:37.237+00:00"}, {"id": "aece14ae-6d67-4906-bcda-80bfeed9fa1b", "raw": {"title": "Oklahoma Corporation Commission Chapter 20 Rules Update", "agency": "Oklahoma Corporation Commission", "summary": "Updated Gas & Hazardous Liquid Pipeline Safety regulations that apply to pipeline operators and service providers in Oklahoma.", "source_url": "https://oklahoma.gov/occ/rules/current-rules.html", "jurisdiction": "Oklahoma (state)", "effective_date": "", "published_date": "2026-08-01", "why_it_applies": "Cascade Pipeline Services (test) provides pipeline inspection, maintenance, and integrity services in Oklahoma."}, "title": "Oklahoma Corporation Commission Chapter 20 Rules Update", "agency": "Oklahoma Corporation Commission", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "Updated Gas & Hazardous Liquid Pipeline Safety regulations that apply to pipeline operators and service providers in Oklahoma.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-08T22:57:33.768213+00:00", "source_url": "https://oklahoma.gov/occ/rules/current-rules.html", "external_id": "9d88191d-191e-4ac4-b8ce-e02f9a954dee:https://oklahoma.gov/occ/rules/current-rules.html", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Oklahoma (state)", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-08-01", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T23:08:02.497+00:00"}, {"id": "07e6c227-9620-4aef-9158-7eb2296c20bf", "raw": {"title": "FTC Finalizes Orders with Cox Media Group, Two Other Firms Settling Charges They Deceived Customers About \\"Active Listening\\" AI-Powered Marketing Service", "agency": "Federal Trade Commission (FTC)", "summary": "Enforcement action against companies that deceived customers about AI-powered marketing services that allegedly listened to customer conversations.", "source_url": "https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/topics/protecting-consumer-privacy-security/privacy-security-enforcement", "jurisdiction": "Federal - United States", "effective_date": "", "published_date": "2026-08-27"}, "title": "FTC Finalizes Orders with Cox Media Group, Two Other Firms Settling Charges They Deceived Customers About \\"Active Listening\\" AI-Powered Marketing Service", "agency": "Federal Trade Commission (FTC)", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "Enforcement action against companies that deceived customers about AI-powered marketing services that allegedly listened to customer conversations.", "category": "regulatory", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-02T22:04:02.560775+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/topics/protecting-consumer-privacy-security/privacy-security-enforcement", "external_id": "898fcee3-a2ba-4bbc-9a47-ae7f1d9d5a14:https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/topics/protecting-consumer-privacy-security/privacy-security-enforcement", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Federal - United States", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-08-27", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": "2026-10-08T00:56:37.237+00:00"}, {"id": "974480ef-fc84-4518-aa5c-75c765510c63", "raw": {"narrative_text": "Litigation Release No. 26659 / September 30, 2026 Securities and Exchange Commission v. Owen E.H. Meyer and Meyer Global Management LLC , No. 26-cv-08607 (S.D.N.Y. filed Sept. 30, 2026) SEC Charges Meyer Global Management and its CEO with Defrauding Retail Investors in Private Funds that Held Interests in SpaceX and Other Pre-IPO Securities On September 30, 2026, the Securities and Exchange Commission charged private fund adviser Meyer Global Management LLC (MGM) and its CEO, Owen E.H. Meyer, with defrauding investors and MGM-managed funds in connection with investments in SpaceX and other pre-IPO securities. According to the SEC’s complaint, in a series of schemes from at least December 2021 to the present, the defendants violated their fiduciary duties by misusing client fund assets, and lied to the underlying investors in the funds. In at least three of those schemes, the defendants allegedly misappropriated client assets from certain MGM-managed funds to pay for Meyer’s personal expenses. As outlined in the complaint, the defendants concealed their actions in one scheme by sending investors statements that inflated their account values. The complaint also alleges that, in one of the misappropriation schemes involving three MGM-managed funds, defendants misappropriated portions of investor proceeds and required investors to sign releases accepting distributions of amounts that were less than they were owed in order to get any money at all. Finally, the complaint alleges that defendants repeatedly failed to address a capital call deficiency owed by an MGM-managed fund in connection with its investment in SpaceX, resulting in the fund forfeiting its nearly $3,000,000 investment. The complaint, filed in the U.S. District Court for the Southern District of New York, charges the defendants with violating antifraud provisions of the Advisers Act of 1940. The SEC seeks permanent injunctive relief, disgorgement of ill-gotten gains with prejudgment interest, and civil penalties against both defendants, as well as a conduct-based injunction against Meyer. The SEC’s investigation was conducted by Heather A. Hosmer of the Enforcement Division’s Asset Management Unit and Victoria Bohannan of the SEC’s Home Office with the assistance of Zachary Scrima, and was supervised by David A. Becker and Corey Schuster of the Asset Management Unit. The litigation will be led by Anna Area and supervised by James Carlson of the Home Office. Resources SEC Complaint Return to top SEC homepage About the SEC Budget & Performance Careers Commission Votes Contact Contracts Transparency Accessibility & Disability Artificial Intelligence FOIA Inspector General No FEAR Act Data Ombuds SEC Excess Personal Property Guidance Websites Investor.gov Related Sites USA.gov Site Information Plain Writing Privacy & Security Site Map Stay connected. Sign up for email updates. Your email address Sign Up X Facebook Instagram RSS YouTube Email Updates", "release_number": "LR-26659"}, "title": "Owen E.H. Meyer and Meyer Global Management LLC", "agency": "Securities and Exchange Commission", "source": "sec_enforcement", "summary": "SEC Charges Meyer Global Management and its CEO with Defrauding Retail Investors in Private Funds that Held Interests in SpaceX and Other Pre-IPO Securities", "category": "enforcement", "countries": [], "created_at": "2026-10-01T00:05:50.589679+00:00", "source_url": "https://www.sec.gov/enforcement-litigation/litigation-releases/lr-26659", "external_id": "LR-26659", "duplicate_of": null, "jurisdiction": "Federal", "effective_date": null, "published_date": "2026-09-30", "on_public_inspection": false, "duplicates_checked_at": null}, {"id": "22d7845c-b771-4e9e-a58b-831837f141ec", "raw": {"title": "Investment Cost Disclosure Simplification - Consultation Published", "agency": "Financial Conduct Authority (FCA)", "summary": "The FCA proposed simplifying how platforms, advisers and wealth managers communicate the costs of investing to consumers.", "source_url": "https://www.fca.org.uk/news/press-releases/financial-regulator-simplify-investment-disclosure-regime", "jurisdiction": "United Kingdom", "effective_date": "", "published_date": "2026-07-02", "why_it_applies": "Brightwell Wealth (test) operates as an investment platform and wealth manager providing financial advice."}, "title": "Investment Cost Disclosure Simplification - Consultation Published", "agency": "Financial Conduct Authority (FCA)", "source": "ai_search", "summary": "The FCA proposed simplifying how platforms, 